## Description

The voice transcript overlay is an opaque box positioned over the chat input container. When a user typed a prompt (or attached context) and then enabled voice mode, the overlay covered the input — hiding both the prompt text and the attachment pills.

Now the input stays open whenever it already has user content, so the prompt/attachments remain visible and editable. The audio-reactive glow still signals the listening state, and the `Listening...` placeholder + live transcript still render when the input is empty.

### Changes

- **`voiceInputDecorations.ts`** (shared sessions overlay): new optional `hasInputContent` observable; when true, the transcript overlay is suppressed instead of covering the input.
- **`voiceInputContent.ts`** (new): `observeChatWidgetHasInputContent(owner, widget)` — an observable that is true when a `ChatWidget` input has trimmed text or attachments, shared by both `ChatWidget`-based surfaces.
- **`chatView.ts`** (active session) and **`chatViewPane.ts`** (main-window chat): wire the helper into their overlays; the `ChatViewPane` guard sits after session-ownership bookkeeping so dictation routing side effects are preserved.
- **`newChatInput.ts`** / **`newChatVoice.ts`** (new-session composer): expose `hasInputContent` on `INewChatVoiceComposer`, combining editor text and `_contextAttachments`, and pass it through.

```ts
// suppress overlay when the user already has a prompt / attachments
if (options.hasInputContent?.read(reader)) {
    transcriptOverlayNode.style.display = 'none';
    transcriptOverlayNode.classList.remove('has-transcript');
    return;
}
```

Note: the dictated `send my prompt` being appended as a suffix (bug #4 in the issue) is intentionally left as-is per maintainer feedback ("ok and by design").

